[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/ced87143-df87-4aaf-9e5f-b2f25342cb7f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {There won't be an Italian game without 1.e4 and 1...e5 on the board! As usual, our knight will occupy its usual f3-square, attacking e5 and controlling the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in a similar way, supporting the pawn on e5. Next, we will play 3.Bc4 — the signature move of the Italian!} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {3...Bc5 is the second most popular move in this position (after ...Nf6), played in nearly 30% of the games. Black doesn't allow us to go for the Fried Liver Attack, but instead brings the bishop out to c5, with similar intentions as our bishop on c4. Our recommendation in this course is to immediately play 4.c3, initiating a simple, but powerful idea of a quick d4-push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Nearly 60% of the games continue with Black bringing the knight to f6. Now it's time for d4. Note that 5.d3 defending the e-pawn is perfectly playable, but we will be looking at the more forcing and aggressive 5.d4 in this course.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black should sensibly trade on d4, as other moves are significantly weaker. This occurs in 90% of the games. We recapture with the pawn, which comes with an attack on the c5-bishop while maintaining our pawn center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {The following studies are divided in regards to what Black does in this moment and on the next move. First, we will look at Bb4+. Black gives a check on b4, which is a move that has been played 75% of the time. We recommend 7.Nc3 here, a very sharp and active approach that doesn't block our dark-squared bishop.} 7.Nc3 7...Nxe4 {Now, 7...Nxe4 is by far the strongest move for Black and also the most common, played almost 60% of the time. Black captures a pawn and piles up on the c3-knight, but that's what we use to our advantage! While Black is occupied by the knight on c3, our king gets to safety and prepares Re1+ against the uncastled king!} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {In 47% of the games, Black captures on c3 with the bishop. We can now gain some space in the center with 9.d5, attacking the knight at the same time.} 9.d5 9...Ne5 {Black immediately saves the knight by jumping to e5 and counter-attacking our c4-bishop. We'll grab the bishop on c3.} 10.bxc3 10...Nxc4 {In return, Black gets our light-squared bishop. To continue playing actively, our next move is Qd4. From d4, the queen has three targets: the pawn on g7 and the knights on e4 and c4.} 11.Qd4 11...Ncd6 {Black saves the knights by retreating to d6. We can now grab the g7-pawn and attack the rook on h8.} 12.Qxg7 12...Qf6 {Black defends with ...Qf8. The queen trade is more or less forced here, as there are no real safe squares for our queen (13.Qg4? runs into ...h5!) After Black recaptures, we'll be able to generate counterplay with Re1+.} 13.Qxf6 13...Nxf6 {Black recaptures on f6 with the knight. Let's give the check we were planning! The lack of Black's dark-squared bishop is apparent here, and Black's queenside is also heavily underdeveloped.} 14.Re1+ 14...Kf8 {...Kf8 is a huge mistake, but played nearly a whopping 70% of the time! We can play Bh6+! to set up a mating net.} 15.Bh6+ 15...Kg8 {The only move.} 16.Re5 {And Rg5+ is a serious threat, which is hard to defend. Black could go for ...Ne4 to stop Rg5+, but then we have Nh4 with f3 coming to kick it out.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/ced87143-df87-4aaf-9e5f-b2f25342cb7f/lines"]

