 the same time.} 9.d5 9...Ne5 {Black immediately saves the knight by jumping to e5 and counter-attacking our c4-bishop. We'll grab the bishop on c3.} 10.bxc3 10...Nxc4 {In return, Black gets our light-squared bishop. To continue playing actively, our next move is Qd4. From d4, the queen has three targets: the pawn on g7 and the knights on e4 and c4.} 11.Qd4 11...Ncd6 {Black saves the knights by retreating to d6. We can now grab the g7-pawn and attack the rook on h8.} 12.Qxg7 12...Qf6 {Black defends with ...Qf8. The queen trade is more or less forced here, as there are no real safe squares for our queen (13.Qg4? runs into ...h5!) After Black recaptures, we'll be able to generate counterplay with Re1+.} 13.Qxf6 13...Nxf6 {Black recaptures on f6 with the knight. Let's give the check we were planning! The lack of Black's dark-squared bishop is apparent here, and Black's queenside is also heavily underdeveloped.} 14.Re1+ 14...Nfe4 {This is the only move which keeps equality, but it is played only in 14% of the games. We'll add pressure to this pinned knight.} 15.Nd2 15...f5 {Black supports the knight with ...f5.} 16.f3 {We'll regain the piece. After a few trades on e4, White will be left with a slightly better bishop than Black. But it's nearly an endgame with opposite-colored bishops, so this position maintains its equality.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/ced87143-df87-4aaf-9e5f-b2f25342cb7f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {There won't be an Italian game without 1.e4 and 1...e5 on the board! As usual, our knight will occupy its usual f3-square, attacking e5 and controlling the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in a similar way, supporting the pawn on e5. Next, we will play 3.Bc4 — the signature move of the Italian!} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {3...Bc5 is the second most popular move in this position (after ...Nf6), played in nearly 30% of the games. Black doesn't allow us to go for the Fried Liver Attack, but instead brings the bishop out to c5, with similar intentions as our bishop on c4. Our recommendation in this course is to immediately play 4.c3, initiating a simple, but powerful idea of a quick d4-push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Nearly 60% of the games continue with Black bringing the knight to f6. Now it's time for d4. Note that 5.d3 defending the e-pawn is perfectly playable, but we will be looking at the more forcing and aggressive 5.d4 in this course.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black should sensibly trade on d4, as other moves are significantly weaker. This occurs in 90% of the games. We recapture with the pawn, which comes with an attack on the c5-bishop while maintaining our pawn center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {The following studies are divided in regards to what Black does in this moment and on the next move. First, we will look at Bb4+. Black gives a check on b4, which is a move that has been played 75% of the time. We recommend 7.Nc3 here, a very sharp and active approach that doesn't block our dark-squared bishop.} 7.Nc3 7...Nxe4 {Now, 7...Nxe4 is by far the strongest move for Black and also the most common, played almost 60% of the time. Black captures a pawn and piles up on the c3-knight, but that's what we use to our advantage! While Black is occupied by the knight on c3, our king gets to safety and prepares Re1+ against the uncastled king!} 8.O-O 8...Nxc3 {This capture occurs close to 45% of the time. Black is likely hoping to relieve the central tension without giving up their bishop pair. We will simply recapture the knight.} 9.bxc3 9...Bxc3 {Black grabs on c3 yet again and attacks our rook on a1: a very natural-looking move. However, ...Bxc3? actually gives us a big advantage! First, let's prevent Black from castling by playing Ba3.} 10.Ba3 10...Bxa1 {Black's greed doesn't end here and they even capture the rook! It is time to punish Black's uncastled king and lack of development. White can start by giving a check with the remaining rook.} 11.Re1+ 11...Ne7 {Black can only block with a piece, since the king cannot go to f8. Let's grab the knight, which is attacked twice by a rook and bishop, but defended by a king and a queen!} 12.Rxe7+ 12...Qxe7 {Black captures with the queen. Now it's time to capture the queen.} 13.Bxe7 {Here, once the bishops on e7 and a1 come off the board, the material is approximately equal by point count, but the position isn't equal at all. Our pieces are MUCH more active than Black's, and White's queen should coordinate with the minor pieces to wreak havoc against Black's open king. These factors should be decisive in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/ced87143-df87-4aaf-9e5f-b2f25342cb7f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {There won't be an Italian game without 1.e4 and 1...e5 on the board! As usual, our knight will occupy its usual f3-square, attacking e5 and controlling the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in a similar way, supporting the pawn on e5. Next, we will play 3.Bc4 — the signature move of the Italian!} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {3...Bc5 is the second most popular move in this position (after ...Nf6), played in nearly 30% of the games. Black doesn't allow us to go for the Fried Liver Attack, but instead brings the bishop out to c5, with similar intentions as our bishop on c4. Our recommendation in this course is to immediately play 4.c3, initiating a simple, but powerful idea of a quick d4-push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Nearly 60% of the games continue with Black bringing the knight to f6. Now it's time for d4. Note that 5.d3 defending the e-pawn is perfectly playable, but we will be looking at the more forcing and aggressive 5.d4 in this course.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black should sensibly trade on d4, as other moves are significantly weaker. This occurs in 90% of the games. We recapture with the pawn, which comes with an attack on the c5-bishop while maintaining our pawn center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {The following studies are divided in regards to what Black does in this moment and on the next move. First, we will look at Bb4+. Black gives a check on b4, which is a move that has been played 75% of the time. We recommend 7.Nc3 here, a very sharp and active approach that doesn't block our dark-squared bishop.} 7.Nc3 7...Nxe4 {Now, 7...Nxe4 is by far the strongest move for Black and also the most common, played almost 60% of the time. Black captures a pawn and piles up on the c3-knight, but that's what we use to our advantage! While Black is occupied by the knight on c3, our king gets to safety and prepares Re1+ against the uncastled king!} 8.O-O 8...Nxc3 {This capture occurs close to 45% of the time. Black is likely hoping to relieve the central tension without giving up their bishop pair. We will simply recapture the knight.} 9.bxc3 9...Be7 {Black plays it "safely" and retreats back to e7. Now we can gain space with d5 and kick the knight out from c6.} 10.d5 10...Na5 {Black jumps to a5, attacking our bishop. Our primary goal should be to open up the e-file against Black's uncastled king. The key move here is thus 11.d6!, also opening up our light-squared bishop, aimed at the soft f7-pawn.} 11.d6 11...cxd6 {Black grabs the pawn on d6. Now, we can sacrifice our bishop on f7! The purpose of this sacrifice is to lure the king out into the open; Black's minor pieces are very passive, which usually indicates that a piece sacrifice is a correct one in the position.} 12.Bxf7+ 12...Kxf7 {Black recaptures. Let's bring the queen into the attack with check!} 13.Qd5+ 13...Kf8 {The king retreats to f8. Now for another key move: we bring the dark-squared bishop to g5, further pressuring Black's pieces and allowing the rook on a1 to join the attack as well.} 14.Bg5 14...Nc6 {Black supports the bishop on e7 by retreating the knight. The knight was also loose on a5, leading to possible tactics if Black's queen was deflected. It is time to bring the a1 rook into the game.} 15.Rae1 15...Bxg5 {Black gets rid of the pressure on the bishop by trading it off. Of course, we would never trade queens here. We'll recapture with the knight, which comes with a checkmate threat.} 16.Nxg5 16...Qf6 {The queen prevents us from delivering a checkmate. Re3! is a strong rook lift, threatening Rf3 to pin the queen to the king.} 17.Re3 17...Ne5 {Black prevents Rf3.} 18.f4 {f4 kicks the knight out from e5. After the knight moves, we'll double up the rooks on the e-file and continue hunting down the king!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/ced87143-df87-4aaf-9e5f-b2f25342cb7f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {There won't be an Italian game without 1.e4 and 1...e5 on the board! As usual, our knight will occupy its usual f3-square, attacking e5 and controlling the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in a similar way, supporting the pawn on e5. Next, we will play 3.Bc4 — the signature move of the Italian!} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {3...Bc5 is the second most popular move in this position (after ...Nf6), played in nearly 30% of the games. Black doesn't allow us to go for the Fried Liver Attack, but instead brings the bishop out to c5, with similar intentions as our bishop on c4. Our recommendation in this course is to immediately play 4.c3, initiating a simple, but powerful idea of a quick d4-push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Nearly 60% of the games continue with Black bringing the knight to f6. Now it's time for d4. Note that 5.d3 defending the e-pawn is perfectly playable, but we will be looking at the more forcing and aggressive 5.d4 in this course.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black should sensibly trade on d4, as other moves are significantly weaker. This occurs in 90% of the games. We recapture with the pawn, which comes with an attack on the c5-bishop while maintaining our pawn center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {The following studies are divided in regards to what Black does in this moment and on the next move. First, we will look at Bb4+. Black gives a check on b4, which is a move that has been played 75% of the time. We recommend 7.Nc3 here, a very sharp and active approach that doesn't block our dark-squared bishop.} 7.Nc3 7...Nxe4 {Now, 7...Nxe4 is by far the strongest move for Black and also the most common, played almost 60% of the time. Black captures a pawn and piles up on the c3-knight, but that's what we use to our advantage! While Black is occupied by the knight on c3, our king gets to safety and prepares Re1+ against the uncastled king!} 8.O-O 8...Nxc3 {This capture occurs close to 45% of the time. Black is likely hoping to relieve the central tension without giving up their bishop pair. We will simply recapture the knight.} 9.bxc3 9...Bxc3 {Black grabs on c3 yet again and attacks our rook on a1: a very natural-looking move. However, ...Bxc3? actually gives us a big advantage! First, let's prevent Black from castling by playing Ba3.} 10.Ba3 10...d6 {Black blocks the scope of the dark-squared bishop on a3 and intends to castle. We'll save our rook with tempo, attacking the bishop.} 11.Rc1 11...Ba5 {Black retreats to a5. Now, a key move: our queen can add pressure to the knight and the bishop at the same time.} 12.Qa4 12...d5 {We are threatening d4-d5, so Black attacks our bishop in return while blocking our d-pawn. However, 13.Bb5 comes and Black's pieces are loose!} 13.Bb5 {Following ...Bb6, Black's position will fall apart after Bxc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/ced87143-df87-4aaf-9e5f-b2f25342cb7f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {There won't be an Italian game without 1.e4 and 1...e5 on the board! As usual, our knight will occupy its usual f3-square, attacking e5 and controlling the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in a similar way, supporting the pawn on e5. Next, we will play 3.Bc4 — the signature move of the Italian!} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {3...Bc5 is the second most popular move in this position (after ...Nf6), played in nearly 30% of the games. Black doesn't allow us to go for the Fried Liver Attack, but instead brings the bishop out to c5, with similar intentions as our bishop on c4. Our recommendation in this course is to immediately play 4.c3, initiating a simple, but powerful idea of a quick d4-push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Nearly 60% of the games continue with Black bringing the knight to f6. Now it's time for d4. Note that 5.d3 defending the e-pawn is perfectly playable, but we will be looking at the more forcing and aggressive 5.d4 in this course.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black should sensibly trade on d4, as other moves are significantly weaker. This occurs in 90% of the games. We recapture with the pawn, which comes with an attack on the c5-bishop while maintaining our pawn center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {The following studies are divided in regards to what Black does in this moment and on the next move. First, we will look at Bb4+. Black gives a check on b4, which is a move that has been played 75% of the time. We recommend 7.Nc3 here, a very sharp and active approach that doesn't block our dark-squared bishop.} 7.Nc3 7...Nxe4 {Now, 7...Nxe4 is by far the strongest move for Black and also the most common, played almost 60% of the time. Black captures a pawn and piles up on the c3-knight, but that's what we use to our advantage! While Black is occupied by the knight on c3, our king gets to safety and prepares Re1+ against the uncastled king!} 8.O-O 8...Nxc3 {This capture occurs close to 45% of the time. Black is likely hoping to relieve the central tension without giving up their bishop pair. We will simply recapture the knight.} 9.bxc3 9...d5 {This is the engine's top choice, equalizing the position. We next improve the rook by giving a check on e1.} 10.Re1+ 10...Be7 {Black saves the bishop and blocks the check. We'll save our bishop as well.} 11.Bd3 {This position is equal. Nevertheless, both of our bishops have good diagonals and we have the semi-open b and e-files to work with. White has good compensation for the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 1: Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line) / Variation 5"]
