 the same time.} 9.d5 9...Bf6 {Many times, Black saves the bishop with ...Bf6. Let's line up our rook with the uncastled king.} 10.Re1 10...Ne7 {Black saves the knight on c6. White simply recaptures on e4.} 11.Rxe4 {Now we have a handful of attacking ideas. Obviously Black is quite solid and smart to reach this far though, so a fight is ahead.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Ne7 {In 39% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. White can add pressure along the e-file with their rook.} 11.Re1 11...Nf6 {In a quarter of the games, Black retreats the knight to f6. The thematic move when Black's knight is on f6 is Bg5; we add pressure to the knight, and here we threaten to double the f-pawns in front of Black's king.} 12.Bg5 12...Ng6 {Black supports the knight by opening up the queen, although this means the knight is pinned. Time to target the knight on g6 again! h2-h4 is the classic way to do this.} 13.h4 13...h6 {Black attacks our bishop in return. But we can just keep advancing! Black can't take on h5 because the f6 knight is pinned to the queen.} 14.h5 14...hxg5 {Black takes the bishop which was pinning their f6-knight. We can take a piece too, but our capture has much graver strategic consequences for Black's position.} 15.hxg6 15...fxg6 {Black recaptures on g6. The g6-pawn and the light-squared bishop had a nice interplay aimed at the f7-pawn. Now, we have an excellent discovered check to play.} 16.d6+ 16...Kh8 {The king moves off the diagonal.} 17.Ne5 {Black's position just collapses after our knight enters a strong outpost on e5, targeting the g6-pawn. The tripled isolated pawns are indefensible, and White's remaining pieces will enter the attack via the h-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Na5 {The most popular move, occurs in nearly half of the games. Black tries to get rid of our strong light-squared bishop. We'll retreat it to d3 and counterattack the e4 knight. ...Nxc3, ...Nf6, and ...Nc5 are the most popular moves, and they all losing! Only ...f5 holds.} 11.Bd3 11...Nc5 {Nearly 30% of the time, Black brings the knight to c5 and tries to get our bishop from there. What might the best move be, considering Black's poor queenside development?} 12.Bxh7+ 12...Kxh7 {Bxh7+ works here as well! Black recaptures, and now we can play the typical Greek gift ideas.} 13.Ng5+ 13...Kg6 {Black steps forward, since ...Kg8 walks into Qh5. We will now bring the queen into the attack. From g4, it threatens a strong discovered check.} 14.Qg4 14...d6 {Black opens up an attack on our queen...} 15.Ne6+ {...but after Ne6+, there will be a checkmate on the next move!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Ne7 {In 39% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. White can add pressure along the e-file with their rook.} 11.Re1 11...Nd6 {In 60% of the the knight retreats to safety on d6 and attacks our bishop. However, this blocks the d-pawn, which hinders their development in the long term. We'll tuck it back the bishop, once again eyeing the h7-pawn.} 12.Bd3 12...Ng6 {Black blocks the view of the bishop. Now, h4! is an aggressive move, intending to kick away he knight from g6.} 13.h4 13...Nxh4 {Black captures on h4, since their queen is also looking over that square. But the Greek Gift sacrifice works here as well!} 14.Bxh7+ 14...Kxh7 {Black captures. It's a simple pattern: we follow up with Ng5+ and then bring the queen into the attack.} 15.Ng5+ 15...Kg6 {Black goes forward to g6, since ...Kg8 runs into Qh5.} 16.Qg4 {But after Qg4 Black's position is hopeless. Discovered check like Ne6+ or even grabbing the knight on h4 is very powerful here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Na5 {The most popular move, occurs in nearly half of the games. Black tries to get rid of our strong light-squared bishop. We'll retreat it to d3 and counterattack the e4 knight. ...Nxc3, ...Nf6, and ...Nc5 are the most popular moves, and they all losing! Only ...f5 holds.} 11.Bd3 11...Nc5 {Nearly 30% of the time, Black brings the knight to c5 and tries to get our bishop from there. What might the best move be, considering Black's poor queenside development?} 12.Bxh7+ 12...Kxh7 {Bxh7+ works here as well! Black recaptures, and now we can play the typical Greek gift ideas.} 13.Ng5+ 13...Kg6 {Black steps forward, since ...Kg8 walks into Qh5. We will now bring the queen into the attack. From g4, it threatens a strong discovered check.} 14.Qg4 14...f5 {Black tries to attack our queen. We can just retreat it to g3 and the threat of Ne6+ is still relevant.} 15.Qg3 {There is no good way for Black to prevent massive material loss from the knight and queen's discovered attacks. If Black tries to run with ...Kf6, we can play Be3 and continue involving pieces into the attack. We also always have the fork with Nh7+ in our back pocket. Black is toast.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Na5 {The most popular move, occurs in nearly half of the games. Black tries to get rid of our strong light-squared bishop. We'll retreat it to d3 and counterattack the e4 knight. ...Nxc3, ...Nf6, and ...Nc5 are the most popular moves, and they all losing! Only ...f5 holds.} 11.Bd3 11...Nxc3 {Black grabs a second pawn, unaware of the danger in the position. Qe1 or Qd2 would skewer the knights and win a piece, but even better is the Greek gift sacrifice! Once again, Black's queenside development is non-existent, which indicates that a sacrifice on the other side of the board is likely sound.} 12.Bxh7+ 12...Kxh7 {Black captures the bishop. Be careful not to play Ng5+? here, as Black can sacrifice their queen with ...Qxg5! because our queen is still hanging. Instead, let's get our queen out of danger with tempo, and involve her in the attack.} 13.Qd3+ 13...Kg8 {Black retreats the king. We can now bring the knight in to threaten a checkmate on h7.} 14.Ng5 {If Black goes for ...f5 or ...g6, we can shift the queen to h3 and threaten a checkmate from there.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Na5 {The most popular move, occurs in nearly half of the games. Black tries to get rid of our strong light-squared bishop. We'll retreat it to d3 and counterattack the e4 knight. ...Nxc3, ...Nf6, and ...Nc5 are the most popular moves, and they all losing! Only ...f5 holds.} 11.Bd3 11...Nf6 {In 34% of the games, the knight retreats to f6 here. Black's loose knight on a5 gives us the opportunity to activate our queen with tempo.} 12.Qa4 12...b6 {Black supports the knight. The real purpose of Qa4 was to swing the queen over to the kingside to support our attack.} 13.Qh4 13...h6 {Black prevents us from adding pressure to the knight by playing Bg5...} 14.Bxh6 {...so let's play Bxh6 instead! Black cannot recapture on h6, since they cannot bring enough forces from the queenside to defend their weak king.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 2: Black Plays 7...0-0 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/b0a7b8ff-f3ba-4d2a-aa55-f8923401ebe0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10.d5 10...Ne7 {In 39% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. White can add pressure along the e-file with their rook.} 11.Re1 11...Nf6 {In a quarter of the games, Black retreats the knight to f6. The thematic move when Black's knight is on f6 is Bg5; we add pressure to the knight, and here we threaten to double the f-pawns in front of Black's king.} 12.Bg5 12...Ng6 {Black supports the knight by opening up the queen, although this means the knight is pinned. Time to target the knight on g6 again! h2-h4 is the classic way to do this.} 13.h4 13...h6 {Black attacks our bishop in return. But we can just keep advancing! Black can't take on h5 because the f6 knight is pinned to the queen.} 14.h5 14...Nh8 {Black tries to play defensively by retreating the knight back to h8.} 15.Bh4 {By retreating the bishop we maintain the pin on f6 and try to make use of Black's heavy underdevelopment.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 3: Black Plays 7...Bxc3+ / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/350bbd7b-8dad-487a-b21a-9ba33e78a90f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the last two studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommended here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...Bxc3+ {Previously we have looked at 7...O-O, but in this line, Black takes on c3, removing the defender of e4 and intending to grab the pawn. This is played in approximately 15% of games.} 8.bxc3 8...Nxe4 {Grabbing some material here and breaking down some of White's center is most sensible as Black. In many positions, if White was allowed to play e4-e5 or d4-d5, it would be very annoying for Black. Typically in this opening, after we sacrifice the e-pawn for development, we CANNOT allow d7-d5, which would stabilize Black's position. Thus, we should play d5 ourselves.} 9.d5 9...Na5 {40% of the time, Black jumps to a5 and attacks our bishop on c4. This is very similar to the previous study where both players have inserted O-O. As before, we should retreat the bishop to d3 with tempo.} 10.Bd3 10...Nxc3 {Black goes for even more material and captures another pawn. In the previous study where both sides castled, we had the Greek gift sacrifice in this position. Here, on the other hand, it is good enough to play Qd2, skewering the knights. We don't mind blocking the dark-squared bishop for now, since it will be fianchettoed to b2 later anyway.} 11.Qd2 11...Qe7+ {Black gives a check on e7. This is necessary to bring the queen to c5 to defend both knights. We should not trade queens and give up our active play, so we move the king to f1! Black still has problems dealing with their hanging knights.} 12.Kf1 12...Qc5 {Black covers the knights with the queen.} 13.Bb2 {And now we fianchetto the bishop to b2, adding another attacker to Black's c3-knight. The pawn on g7 is also very loose, and with Re1+ coming, White is doing great here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 3: Black Plays 7...Bxc3+ / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/350bbd7b-8dad-487a-b21a-9ba33e78a90f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the last two studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommended here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...Bxc3+ {Previously we have looked at 7...O-O, but in this line, Black takes on c3, removing the defender of e4 and intending to grab the pawn. This is played in approximately 15% of games.} 8.bxc3 8...Nxe4 {Grabbing some material here and breaking down some of White's center is most sensible as Black. In many positions, if White was allowed to play e4-e5 or d4-d5, it would be very annoying for Black. Typically in this opening, after we sacrifice the e-pawn for development, we CANNOT allow d7-d5, which would stabilize Black's position. Thus, we should play d5 ourselves.} 9.d5 9...Na5 {40% of the time, Black jumps to a5 and attacks our bishop on c4. This is very similar to the previous study where both players have inserted O-O. As before, we should retreat the bishop to d3 with tempo.} 10.Bd3 10...Nf6 {The knight retreats back to f6 instead of grabbing the d5-pawn, which would give White active development. Here, we can simply castle.} 11.O-O 11...O-O {Black castles as well, and the position has transposed into a line from the previous study.} 12.Qa4 {And we've arrived at an already familiar position, where we first attack the knight on a5 and next slide the queen to h4 to initiate a strong attack!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 3: Black Plays 7...Bxc3+ / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/350bbd7b-8dad-487a-b21a-9ba33e78a90f/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the last two studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommended here is to block with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...Bxc3+ {Previously we have looked at 7...O-O, but in this line, Black takes on c3, removing the defender of e4 and intending to grab the pawn. This is played in approximately 15% of games.} 8.bxc3 8...Nxe4 {Grabbing some material here and breaking down some of White's center is most sensible as Black. In many positions, if White was allowed to play e4-e5 or d4-d5, it would be very annoying for Black. Typically in this opening, after we sacrifice the e-pawn for development, we CANNOT allow d7-d5, which would stabilize Black's position. Thus, we should play d5 ourselves.} 9.d5 9...Ne7 {In close to 30% of the games, Black went for ...Ne7. Since Black hasn't castled here, the g7-pawn is unprotected, and our queen can find a nice square on d4, from where it attacks both the g7-pawn and the knight on e4.} 10.Qd4 10...Nf6 {Black saves both the knight and the pawn, but at the cost of another tempo that they aren't spending developing. White should continue developing actively with Bg5, threatening to cripple Black's pawn structure.} 11.Bg5 {There is no good way for Black to defend against Bxf6 without further contorting their position. White will often regain the pawn here with positional interest, since Black's kingside pawns will be weak, and their king will be unsafe.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 4: Black Plays 6...Bb6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/28ebd472-9eb7-4cf2-827d-74f17e2c9d7d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the previous studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb6 {In 16% of the games, the bishop retreats to b6, which already gives White some advantage. Nevertheless, it could get a bit tricky to play this correctly as White. In general, we should try to quickly roll Black over by advancing the pawn center; in that vein, the move here is d4-d5.} 7.d5 7...Nxe4 {This happens very rarely (only in 5% of the games in the database), as Black is sacrificing a piece on c6 and aggressively hunting our f2-pawn. Black's attacking forces aren't enough, so we should simply accept the material.} 8.dxc6 8...Nxf2 {Black delivers a fork. Now, it is time to counterattack, starting by getting the queen out of danger with tempo.} 9.Qe2+ 9...Qe7 {Black blocks with the queen, as they don't want to lose the castling rights with ...Kf8. We can throw in another check here by capturing on d7, thus avoiding losing the pawn on c6.} 10.cxd7+ 10...Bxd7 {The bishop recaptures. Remember that we are up significant material, so it is now very acceptable to trade queens.} 11.Qxe7+ 11...Kxe7 {After Black recaptures, we can save the rook by attacking the knight and threatening to win two pieces for a rook.} 12.Rf1 {The position is a bit strange, but White is clearly on top with the extra piece. Natural development will continue with moves like Nc3 and Bg5, and as long as White doesn't blunder material, they should be able to easily take over the endgame.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 4: Black Plays 6...Bb6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/28ebd472-9eb7-4cf2-827d-74f17e2c9d7d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the previous studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb6 {In 16% of the games, the bishop retreats to b6, which already gives White some advantage. Nevertheless, it could get a bit tricky to play this correctly as White. In general, we should try to quickly roll Black over by advancing the pawn center; in that vein, the move here is d4-d5.} 7.d5 7...Ne7 {In 35% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. e4 is still hanging, but notice that our pieces defend d5 twice. Thus, we can continue gaining space and disrupting Black's setup with e5!} 8.e5 8...Ng4 {Black doesn't passively retreat to g8, but jumps to g4 instead, adding another attacker to f2. It's time to push again! We don't have to worry about f2. Black doesn't have enough firepower to generate a successful attack. Meanwhile, d5-d6 opens up our light-squared bishop aimed at the weak f7-pawn.} 9.d6 9...cxd6 {Black can ignore f2 for now and trades on d6. We'll gladly recapture with the pawn, and attack the knight yet again.} 10.exd6 10...Nc6 {Black returns the knight to c6. It's time to continue working to keep Black's king in the center. Qe2+! forces Black to surrender castling rights.} 11.Qe2+ 11...Kf8 {Black sidesteps their king. It is finally time to castle, so we get the king into safety and also protect the pawn on f2.} 12.O-O {If Black allows it, we can go for a tactical shot with Bxf7, since after Ng5+, Black's knight on g4 will be hanging. Regardless, Black's position is miserable due to their stranded king, lack of development, and terrible piece coordination.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 4: Black Plays 6...Bb6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/28ebd472-9eb7-4cf2-827d-74f17e2c9d7d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the previous studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb6 {In 16% of the games, the bishop retreats to b6, which already gives White some advantage. Nevertheless, it could get a bit tricky to play this correctly as White. In general, we should try to quickly roll Black over by advancing the pawn center; in that vein, the move here is d4-d5.} 7.d5 7...Ne7 {In 35% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. e4 is still hanging, but notice that our pieces defend d5 twice. Thus, we can continue gaining space and disrupting Black's setup with e5!} 8.e5 8...Ng4 {Black doesn't passively retreat to g8, but jumps to g4 instead, adding another attacker to f2. It's time to push again! We don't have to worry about f2. Black doesn't have enough firepower to generate a successful attack. Meanwhile, d5-d6 opens up our light-squared bishop aimed at the weak f7-pawn.} 9.d6 9...Nxf2 {Black captures on f2, going for the fork.} 10.Qb3 {The queen-bishop battery creates a higher level threat against the f7-pawn.} 10...Nxh1 {Black goes for the material.} 11.Bxf7+ {Check!} 11...Kf8 {The only move.} 12.Bg5 {And after Bg5, there is tremendous pressure against the e7-knight, ensuring that we'll win Black's queen! Our opponent's lack of coordination and development is yet again fatal.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The c3 and d4 Italian / Study 4: Black Plays 6...Bb6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/74208d2e-70c1-4233-9a81-2a7ebcff6773/studies/28ebd472-9eb7-4cf2-827d-74f17e2c9d7d/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {As in the previous studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb6 {In 16% of the games, the bishop retreats to b6, which already gives White some advantage. Nevertheless, it could get a bit tricky to play this correctly as White. In general, we should try to quickly roll Black over by advancing the pawn center; in that vein, the move here is d4-d5.} 7.d5 7...Ne7 {In 35% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. e4 is still hanging, but notice that our pieces defend d5 twice. Thus, we can continue gaining space and disrupting Black's setup with e5!} 8.e5 8...Ng4 {Black doesn't passively retreat to g8, but jumps to g4 instead, adding another attacker to f2. It's time to push again! We don't have to worry about f2. Black doesn't have enough firepower to generate a successful attack. Meanwhile, d5-d6 opens up our light-squared bishop aimed at the weak f7-pawn.} 9.d6 9...Bxf2+ {if ...Bxf2+...} 10.Kf1 {...Kf1 and suddently all of Black's pieces are loose! We'll win at least one of them.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 1: Black Plays 4...d6  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/aede0b97-d845-426e-8258-0b7bbf1dc142/studies/ccdf9347-25af-4019-b11f-8172d21bc181/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...d6 {A move played about 25% of the time is 4...d6 to lend e5 some more support. This move is significantly less active than ...Nf6, since Black is not pressuring our e-pawn as in other lines. d2-d4 is still the clear continuation here, expanding and breaking in the center.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Most of the time you'll see Black simply trade pawns, but we don't mind seeing this. After our c3-pawn recaptures, we will have two strong pawns in the center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Check! This is played half of the time in the database, but it isn't that good. We block and develop with Nc3, and in this move order, Black has not played ...Nf6 and thus cannot win our e-pawn.} 7.Nc3 7...Nf6 {It's quite possible that we could have a transposition with this move order. We'll castle.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {If the main line is avoided and Black castles, we'll go 9.Bg5.} 9.Bg5 {This pin is powerful because of our Nd5 and Qd3 ideas. We've got moves to increase the pressure, but also to develop all our pieces and support our center. Black will have a tough time fighting our central pawn duo.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 1: Black Plays 4...d6  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/aede0b97-d845-426e-8258-0b7bbf1dc142/studies/ccdf9347-25af-4019-b11f-8172d21bc181/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...d6 {A move played about 25% of the time is 4...d6 to lend e5 some more support. This move is significantly less active than ...Nf6, since Black is not pressuring our e-pawn as in other lines. d2-d4 is still the clear continuation here, expanding and breaking in the center.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Most of the time you'll see Black simply trade pawns, but we don't mind seeing this. After our c3-pawn recaptures, we will have two strong pawns in the center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb6 {This move here is played in a more favorable move order. The reason is that Black has inserted ...exd4 and so they won't be left with the problem of dxe5. We'll play Bb5, which threatens to win the pinned c6-knight with d5.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {Black unpins. Now Nc3 develops further and supports our center. The short-term plan is simple: h3 and castling.} 8.Nc3 8...Nge7 {Developing the knight to e7 helps Black a little because if we play e5 it won't attack their knight like in the ...Nf6 line. We castle.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {They do the same.} 10.h3 {But again, we prevent ...Bg4 and we will continue with Re1 and moves like Be3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 1: Black Plays 4...d6  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/aede0b97-d845-426e-8258-0b7bbf1dc142/studies/ccdf9347-25af-4019-b11f-8172d21bc181/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...d6 {A move played about 25% of the time is 4...d6 to lend e5 some more support. This move is significantly less active than ...Nf6, since Black is not pressuring our e-pawn as in other lines. d2-d4 is still the clear continuation here, expanding and breaking in the center.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Most of the time you'll see Black simply trade pawns, but we don't mind seeing this. After our c3-pawn recaptures, we will have two strong pawns in the center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb6 {This move here is played in a more favorable move order. The reason is that Black has inserted ...exd4 and so they won't be left with the problem of dxe5. We'll play Bb5, which threatens to win the pinned c6-knight with d5.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {Black unpins. Now Nc3 develops further and supports our center. The short-term plan is simple: h3 and castling.} 8.Nc3 8...Nf6 {The knight can go on f6. But since our knight already defends e4, we can simply castle.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles as well.} 10.h3 {And here we prevent ...Bg4. We want to play moves Re1 and maybe even e5 to attack the f6-knight. We've got a clear advantage thanks to our two central pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 1: Black Plays 4...d6  / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/aede0b97-d845-426e-8258-0b7bbf1dc142/studies/ccdf9347-25af-4019-b11f-8172d21bc181/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...d6 {A move played about 25% of the time is 4...d6 to lend e5 some more support. This move is significantly less active than ...Nf6, since Black is not pressuring our e-pawn as in other lines. d2-d4 is still the clear continuation here, expanding and breaking in the center.} 5.d4 5...Bb6 {Less than 10% of games continue with ...Bb6; Black retreats the bishop and hopes to keep it exerting pressure on our dark squares. After 6.dxe5, we are almost always simply winning a pawn. Most games (about 80%) continue with 6...Nxe5 or 6...dxe5, but both of those moves are terrible, since after trading queens, either the e5 or f7 pawns drop at the end of the line.} 6.dxe5 6...Bg4 {Bg4, the best move, is only played in around 13% of games in the database. The best move for White here is to chop on d6 and grab the extra pawn. Note that 6...Bg4 is only roughly equal after 7.exd6 Nf6!?, with very strange complications. The majority of players aren't aware of these dynamics.} 7.exd6 7...cxd6 {Most players simply recapture on d6, not knowing d6 becomes a weakness and that they lose valuable time. White should now castle, bringing their king to safety and enjoying their extra pawn and better position.} 8.O-O 8...Ne5 {Perhaps Black's only idea to create counterplay, but it doesn't work. 9.Be2 is also fine, but even better is throwing in a check while saving the bishop.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Kf8 {If Black is stubborn and they don't want to block the check, they'll have to lose castling rights. Now, we can bring the bishop back to break the pin.} 10.Be2 {Up a pawn with a better position, all we must do now is finish development. Na3 and Nc2 is a good maneuver to then pivot to e3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 1: Black Plays 4...d6  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/aede0b97-d845-426e-8258-0b7bbf1dc142/studies/ccdf9347-25af-4019-b11f-8172d21bc181/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4.c3 4...d6 {A move played about 25% of the time is 4...d6 to lend e5 some more support. This move is significantly less active than ...Nf6, since Black is not pressuring our e-pawn as in other lines. d2-d4 is still the clear continuation here, expanding and breaking in the center.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Most of the time you'll see Black simply trade pawns, but we don't mind seeing this. After our c3-pawn recaptures, we will have two strong pawns in the center.} 6.cxd4 6...Bb4+ {Check! This is played half of the time in the database, but it isn't that good. We block and develop with Nc3, and in this move order, Black has not played ...Nf6 and thus cannot win our e-pawn.} 7.Nc3 7...Bg4 {The bishop pins our f3-knight. We can simply castle here and start using our lead in development to cause problems for Black.} 8.O-O 8...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and pressures e4. The position seems stable for Black, but it actually is not. Now Nd5! is a tricky move: we can attack the bishop, and all the continuations favor White.} 9.Nd5 9...Nxd5 {For instance if Black trades, we open the e-file with tempo. More importantly, once the c6 knight is deflected, we will have Qa4+, winning the bishop.} 10.exd5 10...Bxf3 {Black may try and take on f3, but we won't recapture with the queen.} 11.gxf3 {The double isolated pawns are a bit ugly, but they are a small price to pay for White's imminent material gain.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 and 4...Qf6  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/aede0b97-d845-426e-8258-0b7bbf1dc142/studies/00a3d0d9-6625-40b3-a645-221b6edb3cbb/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 4.c3 4...Qf6 5.d4 5...exd4 6.e5 6...Nxe5 7.Qe2 7...d6 8.cxd4 *

[Event "Chapter 2: Sidelines After 3...Bc5 / Study 2: Black Plays 4...h6 and 4...Qf6  / Variation 3"]
