1.e4 1...e5 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 4.c3 4...h6 5.b4 5...Bb6 6.b5 6...Na5 7.Bxf7+ 7...Kxf7 8.Nxe5+ 8...Ke6 9.Qh5 9...Qf6 10.d4 *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 3...h6  / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/ec0702f4-1739-4774-8e34-6478ca44d89b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here, to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...h6 {3...h6 is surprisingly played 13% of the time here. At such an early stage of the game, this includes more than 32 million games! Still, if the idea is to avoid a Fried Liver at all costs, 3...Bc5 is better. Because Black has essentially wasted a move, we should strike immediately in the center with d2-d4; there is no reason to prepare it with c3 here.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {About 70% of the games in the database continue like this, with Black opening the position. We should recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nxd4 {If this capture happens, we've got everything we wanted in the position. After 6.Qxd4, we have a massive lead in development and unopposed central control.} 6.Qxd4 6...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. Since our position has already been opened thanks to our first moves and d4-pawn break, we can simply continue development with Nc3.} 7.Nc3 7...Nf6 {Black develops the knight. We can continue developing our pieces with Bf4.} 8.Bf4 8...Be7 {Black prepares to castle. We can castle queenside here and use our dominating position to start an attack!} 9.O-O-O 9...O-O {Black castles, but their king is now on a lonely side of the board.} 10.h4 {10.e5 or 10.f3 were also options, but I like 10.h4 to attack. This may make many players with Black nervous, and spend some time thinking of how to defend. f3 is an idea that we could also go for later, and Qd2 to sacrifice on h6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 3...h6  / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/ec0702f4-1739-4774-8e34-6478ca44d89b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here, to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...h6 {3...h6 is surprisingly played 13% of the time here. At such an early stage of the game, this includes more than 32 million games! Still, if the idea is to avoid a Fried Liver at all costs, 3...Bc5 is better. Because Black has essentially wasted a move, we should strike immediately in the center with d2-d4; there is no reason to prepare it with c3 here.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {About 70% of the games in the database continue like this, with Black opening the position. We should recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bc5 {This move loses on the spot.} 6.Bxf7+ {With the idea of Qh5+. If Black doesn't take the bishop, we can simply trade down. White will be up a pawn and will enjoy hunting Black's king, which can no longer castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 3...h6  / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/ec0702f4-1739-4774-8e34-6478ca44d89b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here, to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...h6 {3...h6 is surprisingly played 13% of the time here. At such an early stage of the game, this includes more than 32 million games! Still, if the idea is to avoid a Fried Liver at all costs, 3...Bc5 is better. Because Black has essentially wasted a move, we should strike immediately in the center with d2-d4; there is no reason to prepare it with c3 here.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {About 70% of the games in the database continue like this, with Black opening the position. We should recapture with the knight.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nf6 {5...Nf6 attacks e4. We can defend naturally with 6.Nc3.} 6.Nc3 6...Bb4 {Black pins our knight, and thus rethreatens ...Nxe4. We can start by trading the other knights and relieving some tension.} 7.Nxc6 7...bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures. We should castle now. With our king in safety, it'll be harder to win a pawn on e4.} 8.O-O 8...Bxc3 {Black takes. They may feel like they have to at this point. After recapturing, our plan is to play e5 to dislodge the knight. It's dangerous for Black to take on e4.} 9.bxc3 9...Nxe4 {If they take on e4, we have some ways to create problems.} 10.Ba3 {We start taking control of the dark squares around the king. We also stop them from castling here. The best continuation for Black is 10...d5 11.Bb3 Nd6. But in that case we can attack c6 with 12.Ba4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Black Plays 3...h6  / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/ec0702f4-1739-4774-8e34-6478ca44d89b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here, to enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...h6 {3...h6 is surprisingly played 13% of the time here. At such an early stage of the game, this includes more than 32 million games! Still, if the idea is to avoid a Fried Liver at all costs, 3...Bc5 is better. Because Black has essentially wasted a move, we should strike immediately in the center with d2-d4; there is no reason to prepare it with c3 here.} 4.d4 4...d6 {About 12% of games continue with 4...d6. Black defends e5, but the simple capture on e5 is good.} 5.dxe5 5...Bg4 {Black recaptures on e5 in 80% of games. In the previous 3...Bc5 lines, we saw that recapturing the pawn on e5 is lost due to Qxd8+ and Bxf7! ideas. The only move to save the game is once again ...Bg4, and even then, the defense is not obvious. In less than 20% of games, players managed to find this move. Obviously, we can now take on d6 and be up a pawn.} 6.exd6 6...Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. It's more active on the d6-square in this line because it helps control the center. We should start by forcing Black to making a choice with the light-squared bishop.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {If they keep the pin, that's fine. Our plan will be to play g4 later. First, though, we can pin their knight to avoid Ne5.} 8.Bb5 8...Ne7 {Black supports their knight with the other one. We have some time to develop here. Nc3 supports our center.} 9.Nc3 9...O-O {Black castles. ...Ne5 is enabled. It is time to play g4. There won't be significant weaknesses that Black will be able to take advantage of, especially since we haven't castled yet. This expansion lets us play actively and aggressively.} 10.g4 10...Bg6 {The bishop retreats to g6. There's some pressure on e4, but it's currently defended.} 11.Be3 {We're going to play Qe2, Bd3 in some variations if we need to defend e4, and castle queenside. Rg1 is also an idea to push our kingside pawns. White stands better due to the space advantage and the extra pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nd4 and 3...f5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/3cd5f631-3917-4a9b-88da-3f7315737b7a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here and enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...f5 {This is the Rousseau Gambit. If we accept the pawn, Black gets full compensation and good attacking chances with ...e4. Instead, we'll push with d4.} 4.d4 4...fxe4 {Black can take on e4. This is the most popular move. We can counter by grabbing the pawn on e5, opening up our queen in the process.} 5.Nxe5 5...d5 {5...d5 is one of the best moves under the circumstances for Black. We can simply pin the knight.} 6.Bb5 6...Qd6 {Black defends the knight using their queen.} 7.c4 {We simply are too ahead in development and activity. 7...dxc4 8.Nc3 attacks e4. We will simply be getting turbo development and Black is in trouble. They won't be able to defend everything.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nd4 and 3...f5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/3cd5f631-3917-4a9b-88da-3f7315737b7a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here and enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...f5 {This is the Rousseau Gambit. If we accept the pawn, Black gets full compensation and good attacking chances with ...e4. Instead, we'll push with d4.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black can also choose to take on d4. We should recapture with the knight, which also opens up our queen.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nf6 {5...Nf6 covers our queen's checking square. We'll play 6.Nc3 to support our e4-pawn.} 6.Nc3 6...fxe4 {Black takes on e4.} 7.Bg5 {We have maximum pressure on e4 and by extension on Black's entire position. We're going to castle. f3 to open up our rook is also a powerful idea to increase pressure on the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nd4 and 3...f5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/3cd5f631-3917-4a9b-88da-3f7315737b7a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here and enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...Nd4 {This is the Blackburne Gambit. If we take on e5, ...Qg5 is good for Black (a famous trap). So instead, we simply trade knights and double Black's pawns.} 4.Nxd4 4...exd4 {Black recaptures.} 5.O-O {And we castle. We'll be calm about this, and play slow, improving moves like c3 and Re1.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Nd4 and 3...f5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6cdd46e2-7bff-4f8b-bfcf-aeddd9745e6c/chapters/bae7962a-ea1a-4969-9c77-62512ecf3667/studies/3cd5f631-3917-4a9b-88da-3f7315737b7a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here and enter the Italian.} 3.Bc4 3...f5 {This is the Rousseau Gambit. If we accept the pawn, Black gets full compensation and good attacking chances with ...e4. Instead, we'll push with d4.} 4.d4 4...fxe4 {Black can take on e4. This is the most popular move. We can counter by grabbing the pawn on e5, opening up our queen in the process.} 5.Nxe5 5...Nxe5 {Black trades knights. This is already a huge mistake.} 6.dxe5 {It's very hard to stop Qd5. The engine gives us a +2 advantage.} *